NEW and DPoP clients obtain short-lived WebSocket tickets through authenticated HTTP so long-lived tokens stay out of socket URLs. A ticket names the route it opens: the RPC socket, or the media routes (device hub, preview stream), which share one ticket across `<img>`, `EventSource` and several sockets. A DPoP client's RPC ticket is bound to its proof key and the upgrade carries a fresh proof in the `dpop` query parameter, since a WebSocket cannot set headers; a ticket captured on a plain HTTP LAN route is useless without the key, and the proof's `jti` is consumed like any other. Media tickets stay bearer because each media request would need its own proof. Clients that predate this mint unbound tickets that open either route. Browser sessions can authenticate the upgrade with their cookie. A successful handshake grants no extra authority: [every RPC
DIFF replace
